<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Testament</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Testament is the second part of the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, a collection of 27 books in Greek written in the 1st and early 2nd centuries, including the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, the letters of Paul and Revelation. Its translations shaped many languages: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> translated it into German at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> in 1521–1522, and Danish mission scholars at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> published a Tamil version in 1714.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-testament.jpg" alt="New Testament" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ann_Arbor,_University_of_Michigan_Ms_P.Mich.inv._1570_(Papyrus_37)_recto_Matt_26,_19-37.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The New Testament was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> by various authors, although the historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is generally agreed to have spoken primarily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. Its books are thought to have been written by Jewish Christians living in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> under Roman occupation. The author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> is frequently regarded as an exception, and scholars are divided on whether he was a Gentile or a Hellenistic Jew.</p><p>Literary analysis suggests that many of its texts were written in the mid-to-late first century, and there is no scholarly consensus on the date of the latest text. Some scholars date many or all of the books before 70 CE, while others date some texts much later. Richard Pervo dated Luke–Acts to about 115 CE, and David Trobisch places Acts in the mid-to-late second century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The collection has 27 texts: four gospels, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>, epistles attributed to Paul and other authors, and the Book of Revelation. Each gospel narrates the life, death and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resurrection-of-jesus">resurrection of Jesus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>. The word gospel derives from the Old English gōd-spell, meaning good news, and the Greek equivalent is euangélion. Beginning in the late second century the four accounts were called the Gospel according to a named author. The first author to name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a> explicitly was Irenaeus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, in his book Against Heresies, written around 180.</p><p>The order of the books differs between traditions. In the Latin West, before the Vulgate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> stood in the order Matthew, John, Luke and Mark. An early edition of Paul's letters was arranged from longest to shortest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrews</a> was placed in different positions within it. The 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luther-bible">Luther Bible</a> places Hebrews, James, Jude and Revelation last, which reflects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s views on their canonicity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The canon developed gradually. In the first centuries many books were widely considered inspired, but no single canon was formally recognised. About 140 CE Marcion accepted only a version of Luke and ten of Paul's letters, and rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> entirely. Most other Christians rejected his canon, and scholars such as Adolf von Harnack and David Trobisch have argued that the church formed its own canon partly in response to him. The 27-book canon was formalised by the late fourth century.</p><p>The text was transmitted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, now numbering in the thousands, which contain variants. The earliest date from the late second to early third centuries, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> 52 a possible exception. Textual criticism uses them to reconstruct the earliest recoverable text. Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, evangelicals and many other Protestants regard it as inspired scripture. Catholics and Orthodox receive it together with sacred tradition, whereas Protestants hold that doctrine is to be derived from scripture alone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Testament” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Attic dialect became the basis of the koine, the common Greek of the Hellenistic world and of the <b>New Testament</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick the Wise, who had founded the university where Luther taught, refused to hand him over after the Diet of Worms in 1521 and hid him in the Wartburg, where Luther translated the <b>New Testament</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The first printed <b>New Testament</b> in Irish appeared in 1602.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The seer Epimenides, traditionally said to be a Knossian, was remembered for purifying Athens after the sacrilege of the Cylonian conspiracy, and a line attributed to him, that Cretans are always liars, is quoted in the <b>New Testament</b> letter to Titus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of what is known of him comes from the Gospels of the <b>New Testament</b>, written in the decades after his death, with brief references in Roman and Jewish writers such as Tacitus and Josephus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The full canon is described as taking in the Old and <b>New Testaments</b>, the Quran and works of Plato and of those influenced by Socrates, and Druze tradition counts Pythagoras, Plato and Aristotle among the five great prophets of the age of the ancient Greeks.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
